time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="+ LDFLAGS='-Wl,-z,relro -Wl,--as-needed  -Wl,-z,now -specs=/usr/lib/rpm/azl/default-hardened-ld -Wl,-dT,/usr/src/azl/BUILD/module_info.ld'"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="+ export LDFLAGS"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="+ LT_SYS_LIBRARY_PATH=/usr/lib:"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="+ export LT_SYS_LIBRARY_PATH"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="+ PATH=/usr/lib/ccache:/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="+ export PATH"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="+ CCACHE_DIR=/ccache-dir"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="+ export CCACHE_DIR"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="+ CCACHE_COMPILERCHECK=content"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="+ export CCACHE_COMPILERCHECK"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="+ ccache --zero-stats"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="Statistics zeroed"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="+ cd EV-4.34"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="+ PERL_CANARY_STABILITY_NOPROMPT=1"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="+ perl Makefile.PL INSTALLDIRS=vendor 'OPTIMIZE=-O2 -g -pipe -Wall -Werror=format-security -Wp,-D_FORTIFY_SOURCE=2 -Wp,-D_GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS -fexceptions -fstack-protector-strong -grecord-gcc-switches -specs=/usr/lib/rpm/azl/default-hardened-cc1    -fcommon -m64 -mtune=generic -fasynchronous-unwind-tables -fstack-clash-protection -fcf-protection' NO_PACKLIST=1 NO_PERLLOCAL=1"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="*** Canary::Stability COMPATIBILITY AND SUPPORT CHECK"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="*** ================================================="
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="*** Hi!"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="*** I do my best to provide predictable and reliable software."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="*** However, in recent releases, P5P (who maintain perl) have been"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="*** introducing regressions that are sometimes subtle and at other times"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="*** catastrophic, often for personal preferences with little or no concern"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="*** for existing code, most notably CPAN."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="*** For this reason, it has become very hard for me to maintain the level"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="*** of reliability and support I have committed myself to in the past, at"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="*** least with some perl versions: I simply can't keep up working around new"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="*** bugs or gratituous incompatibilities, and in turn you might suffer from"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="*** unanticipated problems."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="*** Therefore I have introduced a support and compatibility check, the results"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="*** of which follow below, together with a FAQ and some recommendations."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="*** This check is just to let you know that there might be a risk, so you can"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="*** make judgement calls on how to proceed - it will not keep the module from"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="*** installing or working."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="*** The stability canary says: (nothing, it was driven away by harsh weather)"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="*** It seems you are running perl version 5.038002, likely the \"official\" or"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="*** \"standard\" version. While there is nothing wrong with doing that,"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="*** standard perl versions 5.022 and up are not supported by EV."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="*** While this might be fatal, it might also be all right - if you run into"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="*** problems, you might want to downgrade your perl or switch to the"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="*** stability branch."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="*** If everything works fine, you can ignore this message."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="*** Stability canary mini-FAQ:"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="*** Do I need to do anything?"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="***    With luck, no. While some distributions are known to fail"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="***    already, most should probably work. This message is here"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="***    to alert you that your perl is not supported by EV,"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="***    and if things go wrong, you either need to downgrade, or"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="***    sidegrade to the stability variant of your perl version,"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="***    or simply live with the consequences."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="*** What is this canary thing?"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="***    It's purpose is to check support status of EV with"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="***    respect to your perl version."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="*** What is this \"stability branch\"?"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="***    It's a branch or fork of the official perl, by schmorp, to"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="***    improve stability and compatibility with existing modules."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="*** How can I skip this prompt on automated installs?"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="***    Set PERL_CANARY_STABILITY_NOPROMPT=1 in your environment."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="***    More info is in the Canary::Stability manpage."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="*** Long version of this FAQ: http://stableperl.schmorp.de/faq.html"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="*** Stability Branch homepage: http://stableperl.schmorp.de/"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:30Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="*** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** ***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="Welcome to EV configuration. If you are in a hurry, just press return here"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="and hope for the best. The defaults should usually do."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="*** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** ***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="POSIX optionally offers support for a monotonic clock source. EV"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="can take advantage of this clock source to detect time jumps more"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="reliably. Unfortunately, some systems are bound to be broken, so you can"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="disable this here: you can completely disable the detection and use of"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="the monotonic clock by answering 'n' here. Support for this clock type"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="will otherwise be autodetected at both compile- and runtime. (this setting"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="currently affects the use of nanosleep over select as well)."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="Enable optional support for CLOCK_MONOTONIC (y/n)? [y] y"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="*** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** ***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="POSIX optionally offers support for a (potentially) high-resolution"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="realtime clock interface. In a good implementation, using it is faster"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="than the normal method of using gettimeofday. Unfortunately, this option"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="is also bound to be broken on some systems, and current EV versions do not"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="actually call gettimeofday very often, so it defaults to no."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="Prefer clock_gettime (CLOCK_REALTIME) over gettimeofday (y/n)? [n] n"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="*** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** ***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="EV can use various backends with various portability issues. The select"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="backend is the most portable and makes for a good fallback, but it can be"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="limited to a low number of file descriptors and/or might not compile. If"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="you have problems with compiling ev_select.c, you might try to play around"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="with disabling it here, or forcing it to use the fd_set provided by your"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="OS, via the next question. I highly recommend keeping it in."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="Enable select backend (y/n)? [y] y"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="*** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** ***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="The select backend can operate in two modes. One uses the system-provided"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="fd_set and is usually limited to 1024 file descriptors (64 on windows),"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="the other requires your header files to define NFDBITS and declare a"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="suitable fd_mask type. If you run into problems compiling ev_select.c, you"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="can try forcing the use of the system fd_set here."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="Force use of system fd_set for select backend (y/n)? [n] n"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="*** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** ***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="The second very portable backend is poll(2). It does not exist on windows"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="and various versions of Mac OS X (and on the other versions it simply"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="doesn't work), but works basically everywhere else. It is recommended to use"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="the default here unless you run into compilation problems in ev_poll.c."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="Enable poll backend (y/n)? [y] y"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="*** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** ***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="Select and poll make it hard to write efficient servers, especially if the"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="number of active connections is much lower than the watched ones. GNU/Linux"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="systems have a more scalable method called \"epoll\", which EV can use. For"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="this to work, both your kernel and glibc have to support epoll, but if you"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="can compile it, the detection will be done at runtime, and EV will safely"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="fall back to using select when epoll isn't available. If unsure, accept"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="the default."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="Enable epoll backend (y/n)? [y] y"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="*** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** ***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="Linux 4.18 introduced another event polling interface, this time using"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="the Linux AIO API. While this API is far superior to epoll and almost"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="rivals kqueue, it also suffers from the same issues as kqueue typically"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="does: only a subset of file types are supported (as of 4.19, I have seen"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="eventfd, pipes, sockets files and some devices, but no ttys). It also"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="is subject arbitrary system-wide limits imposed on it. Therefore, this"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="backend is not used by default, even when it is compiled in, and you have"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="to request it explicitly, e.g. with LIBEV_FLAGS=64. If unsure, accept the"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg=default.
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="Enable linux aio backend (y/n)? [y] y"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="*** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** ***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="The previously mentioned Linux AIO backend is experimental and will not"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="be used unless requested explicitly. You can, howeer, choose to make ti a"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="recommended basckend, which means it will be chosen if available even when"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="not explicitly asked for, in preference to epoll on GNU/Linux. This option"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="is likely temporary. When unsure, accept the default."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="Treat linux aio as a recommended backend (y/n)? [n] n"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="*** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** ***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="Linux 4.19 introduced another event polling interface, \"io_uring\". While"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="this API is far superior to epoll and almost rivals linuxaio, it also"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="suffers from the same issues as kqueue typically does: only a subset of"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="file types are supported (as of 5.2). It is also very buggy still, and"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="most importantly, very very slow for most workloads.  Therefore, this"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="backend is not used by default, even when it is compiled in, and you have"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="to request it explicitly, e.g. with LIBEV_FLAGS=128. If unsure, accept the"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg=default.
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="Enable linux io_uring backend (y/n)? [y] y"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="*** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** ***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="The previously mentioned Linux io_uring is experimental and will not be"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="used unless requested explicitly. You can, howeer, choose to make ti a"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="recommended basckend, which means it will be chosen if available even when"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="not explicitly asked for, in preference to epoll on GNU/Linux. This option"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="is likely temporary. When unsure, accept the default."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="Treat io_uring as a recommended backend (y/n)? [n] n"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="*** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** ***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="EV can take advantage of kqueue on many BSD systems. Support for kqueue"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="will be detected at runtime, with a safe fallback to other methods when it"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="cannot be used."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="Note that kqueue is subtly broken on most operating systems, so by default"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="it won't be used on many platforms, but you can still create your own"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="event loop with kqueue backend if you ask specifically for it."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="Here is what we know:"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="NetBSD:  partially working in at least 3.1 and later. Yeah! :)"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="FreeBSD: broken on at least 6.2-STABLE, spotty in later versions,"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="         sockets *likely* work, ptys definitely don't."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="OpenBSD: reports indicate that it likely doesn't work"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="         (similar problems as on FreeBSD)."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="OS X:    completely, utterly broken on at least <= 10.6."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="Enable kqueue backend (y/n)? [n] n"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="*** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** ***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="Similarly to the kqueue backend above, EV can take advantage of the"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="solaris 10 event port interface. Support for event ports will be detected"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="at runtime, with a safe fallback to other methods when it cannot be used."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="Enable event port backend (y/n)? [n] n"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="*** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** ***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="EV needs the functions pthread_atfork and clock_gettime. On most systems"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="you need some special libraries for this (such as -lrt and -lpthread). You"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="can specify additional libraries to provide these calls (and any other"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="required by EV) now, or accept the default."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="On GNU/Linux systems, EV uses the LSB 3.1 __register_atfork function"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="to avoid the dependency on libpthread, and directly uses the clock_gettime"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="syscall to avoid a dependency on librt."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="Extra libraries for pthread_atfork and clock_gettime? [ ]  "
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="*** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** ***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="A backend of a different kind is the Linux inotify(7) interface, which can"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="be used to speed up (and reduce resource consumption) of stat watchers. If"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="you have the include file and libc support for it, it is usually a good"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="idea to enable it, as kernel availability is detected at runtime."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="Enable inotify support (y/n)? [y] y"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="*** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** ***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="Another useful bit of functionality is the Linux eventfd, which is useful"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="for faster signal handling (don't care) and intra-thread communications"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="(more relevant). Kernel support for this will be probed at runtime, but"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="your libc must contain the necessary wrapper. Glibc 2.7 and later should"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="have this wrapper."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="Enable linux eventfd support (y/n)? [y] y"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="*** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** ***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="Another sometimes useful bit of functionality is the Linux signalfd, which"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="is useful for faster signal handling (don't care). Kernel support for"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="this will be probed at runtime, but your libc must contain the necessary"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="wrapper. Glibc 2.7 and later should have this wrapper."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="Enable linux signalfd support (y/n)? [y] y"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="*** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** ***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="Linux kernels can notify userspace about realtime clock timejumps"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="using timerfd. Libev by default will try to take advantage of this if"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="possible. You can completely disable the detection and use of timerfd for"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="this purpose by answering 'n' here. Support for timerfd will otherwise be"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="autodetected at both compile- and runtime."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="Enable optional support for timerfd to detect timejumps (y/n)? [y] y"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="*** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** ***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="Libev contains numerous internal assert() invocations to check for"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="consistency and user errors. These are normally enabled, but most"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="perl builds disable this error reporting mechanism by default. You"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="can re-enable these asserts here. Enabling them might help you catch"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="programming bugs earlier, but might cause a small slowdown. Also, failures"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="will be reported by aboritng your program, instead of throwing a perl"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg=exception.
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="If unsure, enable this if you only use this perl installation for"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="development, and leave it off for use in production environments."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="Make sure assertions are enabled? (y/n)? [n] n"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="*** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** ***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="Very rarely, people want to tweak EV even more, e.g. to exclude"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="or include certain watcher types or backends. This can be done by adding"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="extra -D options here, or via the EV_EXTRA_DEFS environment variable."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="For example, if you run into compilation problems because of missing memory"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="fences (or you just want extra performance), you can tell EV to not support"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="smp and threads via -DEV_NO_THREADS."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="Most people would just press enter."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="Any extra -D options? [] "
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="*** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** *** ***"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="Checking if your kit is complete..."
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="Looks good"
time="2025-03-07T10:05:31Z" level=debug msg="+ /usr/bin/make -O -j8 V=1 VERBOSE=1"
